WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.850
Step four is the lab's only
write: it posts the file to the

2
00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.988
detection rules endpoint, then
reads the rule back by its I D.

3
00:00:07.988 --> 00:00:10.548
It should come back with the file's query

4
00:00:10.548 --> 00:00:13.187
text and status disabled,
so it never runs.

5
00:00:13.187 --> 00:00:17.590
Step five exports everything deployed,
which is the guide's validate step:

6
00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.704
every rule in the repository deployed
with the same query and status,

7
00:00:21.704 --> 00:00:24.663
and nothing deployed that
isn't in the repository.

8
00:00:24.663 --> 00:00:27.438
Step six is the guide's
cross-source query.

9
00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:31.809
It takes the user entities in the
last seven days of alert evidence,

10
00:00:31.809 --> 00:00:36.180
joins the alert info, and counts,
for each user, the distinct service

11
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.313
sources that raised alerts, keeping
users seen by more than one.

12
00:00:40.313 --> 00:00:42.482
An identity seen by the endpoint,

13
00:00:42.482 --> 00:00:46.099
identity and cloud-app sensors
together is a priority.

14
00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:51.037
The guide's pass: the query runs and returns
rows from more than one service source

15
00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:55.900
across the fleet, showing the identity
and cloud-app sources are feeding alerts.

16
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.925
A test tenant with few
alerts may return none.

17
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.550
Step seven backs it out.

18
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.540
The rule is deployed disabled,

19
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.650
which is the guide's rollback
state; leave it so, and disconnect.
